RB01 is an experimental benchmark adapter. Its functional and performance results do not establish suitability for sensitive data or production use.
What runs: popsicle circuit-PSI cardinality from GaloisInc/swanky at 409d1ceb (semi-honest; classical, not post-quantum, base OT).
Parties: `local` runs both roles as threads of this process; `garbler` and `evaluator` run one role each over plaintext, unauthenticated TCP to the address given.
Data: reads only the input files named; writes nothing but the ready file a garbler is given; prints one result line.
Output recipient: the evaluator learns the cardinality; the garbler learns nothing. Both set sizes leak through traffic volume.
Limits: at most 2^20 elements a role; socket I/O times out after --io-timeout-ms (default 30000); memory grows with the set (about 530 MiB for a garbler at 65,536 elements); no other limit is enforced here.
Unresolved: upstream mishandles empty sets and repeated elements, so the input policy refuses them, and malformed input files too (docs/receipts/btungsten-rb/REPORT.md); the probe switches bypass it on purpose.
The constructors initialize separate RNG consumers from the same seed. The reporting seat observed overlapping streams within each party and matches between some generated bytes and protocol traffic. The security consequences remain unverified.


